REFERENCE   1  (bases 1 to 5198)
  AUTHORS   Han X, Yu D, Gu R, Jia Y, Wang Q, Jaganathan A, Yang X, Yu M,
            Babault N, Zhao C, Yi H, Zhang Q, Zhou MM and Zeng L.
  TITLE     Roles of the BRD4 short isoform in phase separation and active gene
            transcription
  JOURNAL   Nat. Struct. Mol. Biol. 27 (4), 333-341 (2020)
   PUBMED   32203489
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Here, we report that, in human cancer cells, BRD4S forms
            nuclear puncta that possess liquid-like properties and that
            colocalize with BRD4L, MED1 and sites of histone H3 lysine 27
            acetylation. BRD4 puncta are correlated with BRD4S but not BRD4L
            expression levels.
REFERENCE   2  (bases 1 to 5198)
  AUTHORS   Wang J, Li GL, Ming SL, Wang CF, Shi LJ, Su BQ, Wu HT, Zeng L, Han
            YQ, Liu ZH, Jiang DW, Du YK, Li XD, Zhang GP, Yang GY and Chu BB.
  TITLE     BRD4 inhibition exerts anti-viral activity through DNA
            damage-dependent innate immune responses
  JOURNAL   PLoS Pathog. 16 (3), e1008429 (2020)
   PUBMED   32208449
  REMARK    GeneRIF: findings reveal a unique mechanism through which BRD4
            inhibition restrains viral infection and points to its potent
            therapeutic value for viral infectious diseases.
REFERENCE   3  (bases 1 to 5198)
  AUTHORS   Nagarajan S, Rao SV, Sutton J, Cheeseman D, Dunn S, Papachristou
            EK, Prada JG, Couturier DL, Kumar S, Kishore K, Chilamakuri CSR,
            Glont SE, Archer Goode E, Brodie C, Guppy N, Natrajan R, Bruna A,
            Caldas C, Russell A, Siersbaek R, Yusa K, Chernukhin I and Carroll
            JS.
  TITLE     ARID1A influences HDAC1/BRD4 activity, intrinsic proliferative
            capacity and breast cancer treatment response
  JOURNAL   Nat. Genet. 52 (2), 187-197 (2020)
   PUBMED   31913353
  REMARK    GeneRIF: ARID1A regulates BRD4 activity in breast cancer.
            Erratum:[Nat Genet. 2020 Jan 31;:. PMID: 32005967]
REFERENCE   4  (bases 1 to 5198)
  AUTHORS   Tai F, Gong K, Song K, He Y and Shi J.
  TITLE     Enhanced JunD/RSK3 signalling due to loss of BRD4/FOXD3/miR-548d-3p
            axis determines BET inhibition resistance
  JOURNAL   Nat Commun 11 (1), 258 (2020)
   PUBMED   31937753
  REMARK    GeneRIF: BET bromodomain inhibitors (BETi) treatment evicts
            bromodomain containing 4 (BRD4) from the forkhead transcription
            Factor3 (FOXD3)-localized MIR548D1 gene promoter, leading to
            repression of miR-548d-3p. Loss of BRD4/FOXD3/miR-548d-3p axis
            enhances JunD/RSK3 signalling and determines BET inhibition
            resistance, which can be reversed by targeting
            EGFR-MEK1/2/5-ERK1/2/5 signalling.
REFERENCE   5  (bases 1 to 5198)
  AUTHORS   Mqoco T, Stander A, Engelbrecht AM and Joubert AM.
  TITLE     A Combination of an Antimitotic and a Bromodomain 4 Inhibitor
            Synergistically Inhibits the Metastatic MDA-MB-231 Breast Cancer
            Cell Line
  JOURNAL   Biomed Res Int 2019, 1850462 (2019)
   PUBMED   31886177
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Compared to vehicle-treated cells, the combination-treated
            cells showed decreased levels of the BRD4, as well as c-Myc protein
            after 48 hours of exposure. In combination, the selective BRD4
            inhibitor, ITH-47, and ESE-15-ol synergistically inhibited the
            growth of MDA-MB-231 breast cancer cells, but not of the MCF-7 cell
            line
